S20 P3 — bidirectionality verification log (ticket #102, epic #98)
Frozen contract: features/propose-grows-up/p3-brainstorm.md (v12)

METHOD
------
A test that passes while the code is present proves nothing on its own — it
may be asserting something the code does not decide. So the production
behaviors this phase adds were verified in BOTH directions: green with the
code present, and RED with the code deliberately broken.

For each behavior below, run from a CLEAN COMMITTED tree:
  1. assert the mutation string occurs EXACTLY ONCE in the target file
     (so the break is precise and cannot silently miss);
  2. apply that one mutation;
  3. run the naming test file under vitest and record the genuine failing
     test names, verbatim;
  4. restore with `git checkout -- <file>`.
After the last entry the whole suite is re-run and `git status --porcelain
-uno` is asserted clean — TRACKED paths only. Untracked files are out of
scope deliberately: the worktree also holds this phase's review artifacts
(prompts and diffs), which are not part of the implementation and are not
mine to commit or remove.

A MUTATION MUST COMPILE. This is not pedantry — it is the failure that
produced the worst entry in an earlier revision of this file. That entry
broke `excludableMachines(catalog)` into a `gated` identifier a refactor had
already deleted, so `ChainBuilder` threw on every render and ALL SIXTEEN
tests in the file went red. Sixteen reads as strong coverage, which is
exactly why it was recorded instead of investigated — but the list included
rows that mount at `unlockedTier: null`, where `gateCatalog` returns the same
reference and gated-vs-ungated cannot possibly be observed. The honest
mutation is the one a real regression would look like,
`excludableMachines(preview?.gated ?? catalog)`, which compiles — and
measured NOTHING, because the only row that could catch it never proposed,
so the `?? catalog` fallback fired. The property is now genuinely pinned by a
row that proposes first, and the entry records 1. This is the second-order
form of the class this log exists to catch: red for the wrong reason.

HAZARD, learned twice the hard way: step 4 restores to HEAD, so the sweep
must only ever run against COMMITTED work — over an uncommitted edit it
silently reverts that edit. (Both times the suite caught it immediately and
the work was recovered; recorded so the next operator does not repeat it.)

The sweep was run from a local scratchpad harness, which is NOT in the tree.
It needs no reconstruction from memory, though: every entry below records its
target file and its exact before/after strings, so the whole sweep is
replayable from this log alone.

RESULT: 48 behaviors exercised — 46 are PINNED (breaking them fails at least
one named test) and 2 are CONFIRMED BEHAVIOURAL NO-OPS, recorded with their
proofs below rather than given a phantom assertion. No behavior is unpinned.

SCOPE OF THE CLAIM — what this log does NOT cover
-------------------------------------------------
The "verified in both directions" claim is scoped to the behaviors LISTED
BELOW; it is not a universal claim over the diff. Excluded:

  a. The TIER select's CSS RULE (`app.css`, `min-width`). The repo has no CSS
     test harness, so the rule was verified by reading the cascade — it had
     collided with the preview rows' depth-marker class, whose later
     equal-specificity rule won, killing the width and imposing the marker's
     accent colour, mono font and letter-spacing on the control.
     The CLASS ATTRIBUTE is a different matter and IS pinned, hard: it is the
     tests' selector, so reverting it reddens NINE rows (entry below). The
     control's NAMING is pinned too — the wrapping <label>, and the absence of
     an aria-label overriding it — and needed no accessibility harness.
  b. The STRUCTURAL two-pass ordering guard ("the schematic group may come
     FIRST"). It guards a shape, not a line: no single-line mutation expresses
     the single-pass alternative, so its bite is against a future refactor
     that resolves refs during the group walk.
  c. One deliberate FORWARD CANARY, labeled as such in the test itself
     ("carries only non-negative integer tiers"): `parseTechTier` forces the
     invariant for every input and this snapshot has no bad values, so it
     cannot fail in either direction today. It exists to catch a future export.
  d. Comments and doc citations.

THE STRUCTURAL REFACTOR, AND WHAT IT DID TO THIS LEDGER
-------------------------------------------------------
The gated catalog rides the `Preview` record, snapshotted by `repropose`
alongside `clockText`, because every consumer was downstream of a live
preview. An earlier revision carried an entry for "the body memo tracks the
tier (not just the catalog)"; that memo no longer exists, so the entry is
gone because the hazard is gone — not because it stopped being checked. Two
entries replace it: "the preview CARRIES the gated world it was solved
against" (four rows) and "the pickers read the SOLVED snapshot, not the live
tier" (one row).

The refactor's headline guarantee is now pinned rather than merely asserted:
a tier change that cannot re-propose — `repropose` returns early on an
unparseable Rate — leaves the pickers and constrained rows describing the
world the proposal was solved in. The TIER `<select>` is the deliberate
exception: it binds the live tier, so it leads the solved world until the
next successful propose. Both halves are asserted in the same row.

TWO CONFIRMED NO-OPS (deliberate, not gaps)
-------------------------------------------
(1) SEAM :441 — `recipeLabel`'s catalog argument in the CONSTRAINED row.
    The frozen spec predicts this and forbids a pin for it; the sweep
    confirms the prediction empirically. `recipeLabel` uses its catalog for
    one thing, `dflt = effectiveDefaultRecipe(catalog, …)`, and that site
    executes only inside `constrainedRaws.map`. A row is constrained exactly
    when `effectiveDefaultRecipe(gated, …) === null`, so under correct wiring
    no option can carry `(default)`. Under the missed edit `dflt` is the
    ungated default D — but constrained implies D is gated out, and the
    options come from `producerRecipesFor(gated, …)`, so D is absent from
    them and the tag still never fires. The site is changed for consistency;
    the `(default)` tag is pinned where it CAN fire, on a normal stage row.

(2) The TIER select's `value` binding
    (`tierOptions.includes(t) ? String(t) : ""`).
    MEASURED: no client-DOM assertion can discriminate it. Rendering the same
    select with `value=""` and with `value="999"` (no matching option) yields
    a byte-identical DOM — value "", selectedIndex 0, per-option `selected`
    flags [true,false,false], identical innerHTML — because React flags
    `option.selected` rather than setting `select.value`, and an unmatched
    value leaves the DOM's own "ask for a reset" algorithm to select the
    first option, which IS "all". The difference is real only in React's
    SERVER renderer, which this client-rendered SPA never takes. The binding
    is kept (it is the specced shape and keeps a server-rendered string
    honest) and the user-visible outcome — an above-range tier displays
    "all", with no write-back — IS pinned; only the expression is unpinnable,
    and no test pretends otherwise. This finding produced spec v12.

